/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sc-300.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sc-300", [["sc1",0,1,"Your company bought the domain contoso.com and wants users to sign in as name@contoso.com. You added the domain in Microsoft Entra ID. What must you do before you can set it as users' UPN suffix?",["Buy a public TLS certificate for contoso.com and upload it to the tenant","Run Microsoft Entra Connect with the domain filtering option","Create a TXT or MX record the admin center gives you and verify it","Open a support request so Microsoft can transfer ownership"],2,"A custom domain must be verified by adding the TXT (or MX) record Microsoft gives you to public DNS. Certificates and sync tools are not part of domain verification.","Configure and manage custom domains",["TLS certificates secure web traffic for a site; Entra ID never asks for one to prove you own a domain.","Entra Connect syncs on-premises AD objects; domain filtering chooses what to sync and doesn't verify domain ownership.",null,"Microsoft doesn't transfer domain ownership by support ticket; you prove ownership yourself through a DNS record."],2],["sc2",0,1,"Regional IT staff in Germany must reset passwords only for German users. Which approach follows least privilege?",["Assign Helpdesk Administrator scoped to a Germany administrative unit","Give them Global Administrator with a PIM approval requirement","Make them owners of a security group containing German users","Assign Privileged Authentication Administrator at tenant scope"],0,"Administrative units scope roles like Helpdesk Administrator to a subset of users. Group ownership does not grant password reset, and the other roles are tenant-wide and far too broad.","Administrative units",[null,"Global Administrator is tenant-wide and far beyond password resets; PIM approval limits when it's active, not how much it can do.","Group owners can manage membership, but ownership gives no rights to reset members' passwords.","Privileged Authentication Administrator can reset passwords for any user, including admins, across the whole tenant, which is much too broad."],2],["sc3",0,1,"You want users added to a group automatically when their department attribute is set to Finance. What do you need?",["A Microsoft 365 group with assigned membership","An access package with automatic assignment after manager approval","A distribution list synced from Exchange Online","A dynamic membership rule and Microsoft Entra ID P1 licenses"],3,"Dynamic membership rules evaluate user attributes such as department and need P1. Assigned membership is manual, and approval-based access packages are not automatic.","Create and manage groups",["Assigned membership means someone adds members by hand, so the department attribute would never add users automatically.","Access packages grant access through requests and approvals; they don't add users just because an attribute changes.","Distribution lists are Exchange mail objects and don't add members by evaluating the department attribute.",null],2],["sc4",0,1,"Which Microsoft Entra feature lets you assign Microsoft 365 licenses to users by adding them to a group?",["Administrative unit license scoping","Group-based licensing","Dynamic license provisioning in Entra Connect","Entitlement management license packages"],1,"Group-based licensing assigns licenses to all members of a group and keeps assignments as membership changes. The other options are not how licensing is assigned.","Manage licenses",["Administrative units scope admin roles to subsets of users; they don't assign licenses.",null,"Entra Connect syncs identities from on-premises AD and has no license provisioning feature.","Entitlement management access packages bundle groups, apps and sites, not license assignments as a separate object."],1],["sc5",0,1,"After a group-based license assignment, several users show an error. The service plans conflict with a license they already had directly. Where do you see and fix this?",["In the sign-in logs, filtered by the licensing service application and user","In the Licenses page for the group, which lists users in an error state","In the provisioning logs of the enterprise application","In Microsoft Entra Connect Health sync errors"],1,"The group's license view shows users with assignment errors such as conflicting service plans or not enough licenses. Sign-in and provisioning logs do not track licensing errors.","Manage licenses",["Sign-in logs record authentication attempts and don't track license assignment errors.",null,"Enterprise app provisioning logs cover SCIM provisioning to SaaS apps, not Microsoft 365 license assignment.","Connect Health reports on-premises sync and AD FS issues, not licensing conflicts in the cloud."],2],["sc6",0,1,"A company wants a small team of admins to manage a set of sensitive executive accounts, and even Global Administrators should not be able to change those accounts unless they are assigned in that scope. What should you use?",["A dynamic group with the executives and a Conditional Access policy","A PIM for Groups assignment for the executive group","A custom role with only microsoft.directory/users/password/update","A restricted management administrative unit"],3,"Restricted management administrative units block tenant-level admins from changing member objects unless they hold a role scoped to that unit. Conditional Access and custom roles do not prevent tenant-wide admins from making changes.","Administrative units",["A dynamic group plus Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions but doesn't stop tenant admins from editing those accounts.","PIM for Groups gives just-in-time group membership or ownership; it doesn't shield the members' accounts from Global Administrators.","A custom role grants its holder a permission; it doesn't take any rights away from Global Administrators.",null],3],["sc7",0,1,"You need a role that can manage users' app assignments for one enterprise app only, without broader rights. Built-in roles are too broad. What can you create?",["A custom role scoped to that application","A new administrative unit that contains the app","A Conditional Access policy targeting that app","A dynamic group that owns the app's service principal"],0,"Custom Microsoft Entra roles can be built from specific permissions and assigned at an application scope. Administrative units hold users, groups and devices, not enterprise apps.","Create custom roles",[null,"Administrative units contain users, groups and devices, not enterprise applications, so they can't scope rights to one app.","Conditional Access controls how users sign in to an app; it doesn't delegate rights to manage app assignments.","Groups can't be made owners of a service principal in this way, and ownership would give more than assignment rights."],2],["sc8",0,1,"Laptops are joined to on-premises Active Directory, and users also need Conditional Access device-based policies in the cloud. Which device identity should they have?",["Microsoft Entra registered","Microsoft Entra joined only","Microsoft Entra hybrid joined","Workplace joined in AD FS only"],2,"Hybrid joined devices stay in on-premises AD and are also registered in Microsoft Entra ID, so they can satisfy device-based Conditional Access. Registered is for personal devices, and Entra joined would remove the on-premises AD join.","Manage devices",["Registered is for personal (BYOD) devices; it doesn't represent a corporate device joined to on-premises AD.","Entra joined only would take the laptops out of the on-premises AD domain they need to stay in.",null,"Workplace join through AD FS is a legacy registration method and doesn't give the full hybrid device identity used by Conditional Access."],1],["sc9",0,1,"Partners from a supplier need to open documents in your SharePoint site. They should appear as guest users you can govern. Which feature fits?",["B2B direct connect","Cross-tenant synchronization","B2B collaboration","Microsoft Entra External ID for customers"],2,"B2B collaboration invites external users as guests in your directory. B2B direct connect creates no guest object and is used for Teams shared channels. Customer (CIAM) tenants are for consumer apps.","External identities",["B2B direct connect creates no guest objects in your directory and is only used for Teams shared channels.","Cross-tenant synchronization is for tenants within the same organization, not an outside supplier.",null,"External ID for customers is for consumer-facing apps in a separate customer tenant, not partners collaborating in SharePoint."],1],["sc10",0,1,"Two business units have separate Microsoft Entra tenants in the same company. They want users from tenant A to be created and kept updated as B2B users in tenant B automatically. What should you configure?",["Cross-tenant synchronization","B2B direct connect trust settings","Microsoft Entra Connect with two forests","A guest invitation bulk upload each week"],0,"Cross-tenant synchronization automatically creates, updates and removes B2B users across tenants in one organization. Bulk invitations are manual, and Entra Connect syncs on-premises AD, not tenants.","Cross-tenant access",[null,"B2B direct connect trust settings allow shared channel access but don't create or update B2B users in the other tenant.","Entra Connect syncs on-premises AD forests into one tenant; it doesn't sync users between two cloud tenants.","A weekly bulk invitation is manual, misses updates and removals, and isn't automatic."],3],["sc11",0,1,"Your tenant should accept MFA already completed by users in a trusted partner's tenant, so guests are not prompted twice. Where do you set this?",["External collaboration settings, guest user access","Authentication methods policy for external users","The partner's Conditional Access policy exclusions","Inbound trust settings in cross-tenant access settings"],3,"Inbound trust settings in cross-tenant access settings can trust MFA, compliant device and hybrid joined device claims from the partner tenant. External collaboration settings control invitations and guest permissions, not trust.","Cross-tenant access",["External collaboration settings control who can invite guests and what guests can see, not whether partner MFA is trusted.","The authentication methods policy decides which methods your users can use; it has no setting to trust another tenant's MFA claims.","The partner's policies apply in their tenant and can't change how your tenant evaluates MFA for guests.",null],3],["sc12",0,1,"Only admins and users in the Guest Inviter role should be able to invite guests. Where is this set?",["Cross-tenant access default outbound settings","External collaboration settings","Conditional Access for guests and external users","Terms of use for external users"],1,"External collaboration settings control who can invite guests and which domains are allowed or blocked. Cross-tenant access settings control B2B access with other Entra tenants, not who can send invitations.","External identities",["Default outbound settings control your users' access to other tenants, not who can send guest invitations.",null,"Conditional Access controls how guests sign in; it doesn't decide who can invite them.","Terms of use makes users accept an agreement; it has nothing to do with invitation permissions."],1],["sc13",0,1,"You are syncing a single AD forest and want the fewest on-premises dependencies, plus leaked credential detection in ID Protection. Which sign-in method should you pick?",["Pass-through authentication","Password hash synchronization","Federation with AD FS","Seamless SSO without any sync"],1,"PHS authenticates in the cloud, so it keeps working if on-premises is down, and it enables leaked-credential detection. PTA and AD FS both rely on on-premises servers for each sign-in.","Hybrid identity",["PTA sends each sign-in to on-premises agents, which adds dependencies, and on its own doesn't give leaked credential detection.",null,"AD FS needs a farm of on-premises federation and proxy servers, which is the most infrastructure of any option.","Seamless SSO is an add-on to PHS or PTA and doesn't authenticate users without synced identities."],2],["sc14",0,1,"Security requires that on-premises account lockout and logon hour restrictions apply the moment users sign in to Microsoft 365, but passwords must not be stored in the cloud in any form. Which method fits?",["Password hash synchronization","Cloud-only accounts with SSPR","Password hash sync with staged rollout","Pass-through authentication"],3,"PTA validates passwords against on-premises AD through agents at sign-in, so AD policies apply in real time and no hash is synced. PHS stores a hash of the hash in the cloud.","Hybrid identity",["PHS stores a hash of the password hash in the cloud, which breaks the requirement that no password data is stored there.","Cloud-only accounts aren't on-premises accounts, so on-premises lockout and logon hours wouldn't apply.","Staged rollout with PHS still syncs password hashes to the cloud; it only changes which users sign in that way.",null],3],["sc15",0,1,"A company acquired another firm whose AD forest has no network connection to the main forest. They want to sync both into one tenant with little infrastructure and highly available agents. What should they use?",["Microsoft Entra Cloud Sync","Microsoft Entra Connect Sync in staging mode","A second tenant and cross-tenant sync","AD FS with a claims provider trust"],0,"Cloud Sync uses lightweight agents that can be installed in disconnected forests, with multiple agents for high availability. Connect Sync needs one server with line of sight to every forest.","Hybrid identity",[null,"Staging mode is a standby Connect Sync server; it still needs line of sight to every forest and isn't a high-availability design.","Creating a second tenant goes against the goal of syncing both forests into one tenant.","AD FS handles federated sign-in, not synchronizing users from a disconnected forest."],3],["sc16",0,1,"You are moving from AD FS federation to cloud authentication and want to test with a pilot group before converting the whole domain. What should you use?",["Microsoft Entra Connect staging mode","Seamless SSO group filtering","Staged rollout","A Conditional Access report-only policy"],2,"Staged rollout moves selected groups to PHS or PTA while the domain stays federated. Connect staging mode is a standby sync server, not a way to change sign-in for pilot users.","Hybrid identity",["Connect staging mode is a standby sync server and doesn't change how pilot users sign in.","Seamless SSO has no group filtering feature for moving pilot users off federation.",null,"Report-only mode tests Conditional Access policies, not a change of authentication method."],3],["sc17",0,1,"By default, how often does Microsoft Entra Connect Sync run a delta synchronization cycle?",["Every 2 minutes","Every 12 hours","Every 30 minutes","Once per day at midnight UTC"],2,"The built-in scheduler runs a delta sync every 30 minutes by default. You can force one sooner with Start-ADSyncSyncCycle -PolicyType Delta.","Hybrid identity",["Two minutes is the interval for Cloud Sync provisioning cycles, not the default Connect Sync delta schedule.","Twelve hours is much longer than the default delta cycle of Connect Sync.",null,"Connect Sync doesn't run once a day; its scheduler runs delta syncs much more often."],1],["sc18",0,1,"Users on domain-joined PCs inside the corporate network should reach Microsoft 365 without typing their password, and you use PHS. Which feature adds this?",["Seamless single sign-on","Passwordless phone sign-in","Microsoft Entra application proxy","Cross-tenant trust for device claims"],0,"Seamless SSO signs users in automatically on domain-joined devices on the corporate network with PHS or PTA. The other features do not provide silent sign-in from domain-joined PCs.","Hybrid identity",[null,"Passwordless phone sign-in uses Authenticator, so users still interact on their phone instead of being signed in silently.","Application proxy publishes on-premises apps externally; it doesn't give silent sign-in to Microsoft 365.","Cross-tenant device trust applies to guests from other tenants, not domain-joined PCs on your own network."],1],["sc19",0,1,"Where can you monitor Microsoft Entra Connect sync errors, such as duplicate attribute conflicts, and get alerts about them?",["Identity Secure Score","Entitlement management reports","The Conditional Access insights workbook","Microsoft Entra Connect Health"],3,"Connect Health shows sync errors (for example duplicate proxyAddresses or UPN) and sends alerts about the sync service. Secure Score and workbooks do not report per-object sync errors.","Hybrid identity",["Identity Secure Score measures adoption of security settings and doesn't list per-object sync errors.","Entitlement management reports show access package assignments, not directory sync errors.","The Conditional Access insights workbook shows policy results for sign-ins, not sync errors.",null],1],["sc20",0,1,"An admin needs to create 400 test users from a spreadsheet in the portal without writing scripts. What should they use?",["Cross-tenant synchronization","Bulk create with the downloadable CSV template","A dynamic group rule","A lifecycle workflow joiner template that imports new users"],1,"Bulk operations in the admin center take a CSV template to create, invite or delete many users. Lifecycle workflows run tasks for existing users and do not import users from a file.","Create and manage users",["Cross-tenant synchronization copies users from another Entra tenant, not from a spreadsheet.",null,"A dynamic group rule adds existing users to a group; it can't create user accounts.","Lifecycle workflows run tasks for users who already exist; they don't import users from a file."],2],["sc21",0,1,"Guests should not be able to list other users and groups in your directory. Which setting controls this?",["Outbound access in cross-tenant access settings","Guest user access restrictions in external collaboration settings","The 'Users can register applications' user setting","The Directory Readers role assigned to all guests through a dynamic group"],1,"Guest user access restrictions decide how much of the directory guests can see, with the most restrictive level limiting them to their own objects. Assigning Directory Readers would give guests more access, not less.","External identities",["Outbound cross-tenant settings control your users' access to other tenants, not what guests can see in yours.",null,"That setting controls whether users can create app registrations, not guests' directory visibility.","Directory Readers lets guests read the directory, which gives them more visibility instead of less."],3],["sc22",0,2,"A 40-person company has no Microsoft Entra ID P1 licenses and wants a baseline that requires MFA registration and blocks legacy authentication. What should they enable?",["A Conditional Access baseline policy","Identity Protection user risk policy","Per-user MFA with app passwords","Security defaults"],3,"Security defaults are free and turn on MFA registration, protect admins and block legacy authentication. Conditional Access and ID Protection need P1/P2 licenses.","Security defaults",["There are no free Conditional Access baseline policies anymore; Conditional Access needs P1 licenses.","ID Protection risk policies need P2 licenses, which this company doesn't have.","Per-user MFA doesn't block legacy authentication, and app passwords actually let legacy clients bypass MFA.",null],1],["sc23",0,2,"You are creating your first Conditional Access policy that requires MFA for all users. What should you do first to avoid locking everyone out?",["Exclude two emergency access accounts and start in report-only mode","Enable security defaults at the same time as a backup","Include only the Global Administrator role for the first week","Block legacy authentication before creating any other policy"],0,"Emergency access accounts excluded from policies, plus report-only testing, prevent a tenant-wide lockout. Security defaults cannot be used together with Conditional Access.","Conditional Access",[null,"Security defaults must be turned off before Conditional Access policies can be enabled, so they can't run side by side as a backup.","Targeting only Global Administrators first risks locking out the very admins who would need to fix the policy.","Blocking legacy authentication is a good policy, but it doesn't protect against lockout from an MFA policy."],2],["sc24",0,2,"Admins must sign in only with phishing-resistant methods such as passkeys or Windows Hello for Business. Which Conditional Access grant control enforces this?",["Require multifactor authentication","Require approved client app","Require authentication strength","Require password change"],2,"Authentication strength lets you require specific methods, such as the built-in phishing-resistant MFA strength. Plain 'Require MFA' also accepts weaker methods such as SMS.","Conditional Access",["Plain Require MFA accepts any registered second factor, including SMS and voice, which aren't phishing-resistant.","Require approved client app checks which client app is used, not which authentication method the admin used.",null,"Require password change is used with user risk policies and doesn't enforce any specific sign-in method."],1],["sc25",0,2,"A new hire has no password and no MFA method yet but must register a passkey on day one. What should the admin issue?",["A one-time SMS code from SSPR","A client secret on an app registration","A Temporary Access Pass","An app password"],2,"A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited code that lets a user sign in and register strong methods. SSPR needs an already registered method, and app passwords are for legacy clients.","Authentication methods",["SSPR can only send codes to a method the user already registered, and this new hire has none.","Client secrets are credentials for applications, not for users signing in.",null,"App passwords are for legacy clients that can't do MFA and can't be used to register a passkey."],1],["sc26",0,2,"You want users who still rely on SMS to be prompted to set up Microsoft Authenticator when they sign in, with a limited number of snoozes. What should you configure?",["A registration campaign","System-preferred MFA","A sign-in risk policy","Combined registration interrupt in SSPR"],0,"A registration campaign nudges users at sign-in to register Authenticator and can limit snoozes. System-preferred MFA picks which already registered method is offered first, but does not ask users to register a new one.","Authentication methods",[null,"System-preferred MFA picks the strongest already registered method at sign-in; it doesn't ask users to register a new one.","A sign-in risk policy reacts to risky sign-ins and doesn't nudge users to set up Authenticator.","Combined registration interrupts ask for missing required methods but don't target SMS users with a limited-snooze Authenticator prompt."],2],["sc27",0,2,"Users with several registered methods should be prompted with the most secure one they have instead of the one they last used. Which feature does this?",["Authentication method registration campaign","Number matching","Sign-in frequency session control","System-preferred multifactor authentication"],3,"System-preferred MFA picks the strongest registered method at sign-in. Number matching is a feature of Authenticator push, not method selection.","Authentication methods",["A registration campaign prompts users to register Authenticator; it doesn't choose which registered method is offered at sign-in.","Number matching makes users type a number in Authenticator push notifications; it doesn't choose between methods.","Sign-in frequency controls how often users reauthenticate, not which method they are prompted for.",null],1],["sc28",0,2,"Hybrid users reset their password through SSPR, but they still cannot sign in to on-premises resources with the new password. What is missing?",["Seamless single sign-on","Password writeback in Entra Connect or Cloud Sync","A custom banned password list","Microsoft Entra hybrid join for every Windows device"],1,"Password writeback sends cloud password changes back to on-premises AD. Without it, the reset only changes the cloud password.","SSPR",["Seamless SSO provides silent sign-in on domain-joined PCs and doesn't send new passwords to on-premises AD.",null,"A custom banned password list blocks weak passwords but doesn't sync password changes to on-premises AD.","Hybrid join is a device identity feature and doesn't write reset passwords back to AD."],2],["sc29",0,2,"The company wants to stop users from choosing passwords that contain the company name or product names. What should you configure?",["Smart lockout threshold and duration","A custom banned password list in Password Protection","A Conditional Access user risk policy","Password expiration of 30 days in the tenant's password policy settings"],1,"The custom banned password list (up to 1,000 terms) blocks those words and variations. Smart lockout protects against guessing, not weak password choices.","Password protection",["Smart lockout limits password guessing attempts but doesn't stop users from choosing weak passwords.",null,"A user risk policy responds to compromised accounts and doesn't check the words in a new password.","Frequent expiration makes users rotate passwords more often but doesn't block company or product names."],2],["sc30",0,2,"You want the banned password lists to apply when users change passwords on on-premises domain controllers too. What must you deploy?",["The Microsoft Entra Connect Health agent for AD DS","Pass-through authentication agents","The application proxy private network connector","The Password Protection DC agent and proxy service"],3,"Password Protection for AD DS needs the DC agent on domain controllers and the proxy service that fetches policy from the cloud. PTA agents validate sign-ins, not password changes.","Password protection",["The Connect Health AD DS agent monitors domain controllers but doesn't enforce banned password lists.","PTA agents validate cloud sign-ins against AD; they don't check passwords when users change them.","The private network connector publishes internal apps and has nothing to do with password policy.",null],3],["sc31",0,2,"Attackers are trying many passwords against accounts, and legitimate users keep getting locked out too. Which feature locks out attackers while trying to let real users keep working?",["Smart lockout","A custom banned password list","Temporary Access Pass","Continuous access evaluation"],0,"Smart lockout uses familiar vs unfamiliar location data to lock out attackers while trying to keep real users working, with a configurable threshold and duration.","Password protection",[null,"A banned password list stops weak password choices but doesn't respond to repeated failed attempts.","A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited sign-in code for onboarding or recovery, not a defense against guessing.","CAE revokes sessions after events like account disable; it doesn't lock out repeated failed password attempts."],1],["sc32",0,2,"Sign-ins from countries where the company does not operate must be blocked. What do you create before the Conditional Access policy?",["A dynamic device group with a region rule","A Global Secure Access remote network","A named location with those countries","A terms of use document per country"],2,"Named locations define IP ranges or countries that Conditional Access can include or exclude. Device groups and terms of use do not represent network location.","Conditional Access",["Device groups group device objects by attributes and can't represent where a sign-in is coming from.","A Global Secure Access remote network connects branch offices to the service; it doesn't define countries for Conditional Access.",null,"Terms of use requires accepting an agreement and doesn't identify or block countries."],2],["sc33",0,2,"A policy should apply only to devices whose model attribute is a specific kiosk model. Which Conditional Access condition supports this?",["Device platforms","Client apps","Filter for devices","Insider risk"],2,"Filter for devices uses device attributes such as model, deviceId or extensionAttributes. Device platforms only matches the OS type.","Conditional Access",["Device platforms only match the operating system type, such as Windows or iOS, not the device model.","Client apps matches browser, mobile/desktop or legacy clients, not device attributes.",null,"Insider risk is based on user behavior signals from Purview, not a device's model attribute."],1],["sc34",0,2,"Users on unmanaged devices should use SharePoint in the browser but be unable to download files. Which Conditional Access control helps?",["Session control: use app enforced restrictions or Conditional Access App Control","Grant control: require approved client app","Grant control: require terms of use","Session control: sign-in frequency every hour for unmanaged devices in SharePoint"],0,"App enforced restrictions (in SharePoint/Exchange) or Conditional Access App Control through Defender for Cloud Apps can allow browser access and block downloads. Sign-in frequency only changes how often users must sign in again.","Conditional Access",[null,"Require approved client app forces specific mobile apps; it doesn't allow browser access while blocking downloads.","Terms of use makes users accept an agreement and doesn't restrict downloads.","Sign-in frequency only changes how often users reauthenticate; downloads would still be allowed."],3],["sc35",0,2,"Admins must sign in again every 4 hours on the Azure portal, even with a valid session. What should you configure?",["Persistent browser session set to never","Continuous access evaluation strict mode","Token lifetime policy for access tokens","Sign-in frequency session control"],3,"Sign-in frequency sets how long before users must reauthenticate. Persistent browser session controls whether the browser session survives closing the browser.","Conditional Access",["Persistent browser session controls whether the session survives closing the browser, not a fixed reauthentication interval.","CAE strict mode enforces location and critical events; it doesn't make admins reauthenticate on a schedule.","Configurable token lifetimes for access tokens don't force a fresh interactive sign-in every 4 hours, and refresh tokens would keep the session alive.",null],1],["sc36",0,2,"A user is unexpectedly blocked from an app. You want to know which Conditional Access policies would apply for that user, app, location and device without them signing in again. Which tool do you use?",["Report-only mode","The What If tool","Identity Secure Score","Access reviews"],1,"The What If tool simulates a sign-in with the conditions you choose and lists which policies would apply. Report-only mode logs results only when real sign-ins happen.","Conditional Access",["Report-only mode records results only when real sign-ins happen, so you'd have to wait for the user to sign in again.",null,"Identity Secure Score measures overall security posture and doesn't evaluate policies for one user's sign-in.","Access reviews confirm whether users still need access; they don't show which Conditional Access policies apply."],2],["sc37",0,2,"Which risk type is most closely tied to leaked credentials found by Microsoft?",["Sign-in risk","User risk","Service principal risk","Device risk"],1,"Leaked credentials is a user risk detection, because it says the account itself may be compromised, not one sign-in.","ID Protection",["Sign-in risk is about one specific sign-in looking suspicious, while leaked credentials concern the account itself.",null,"Service principal risk applies to workload identities, not leaked credentials of user accounts.","Device risk comes from endpoint signals like Defender for Endpoint, not from leaked credential detections."],1],["sc38",0,2,"When sign-in risk is medium or high, users should be able to continue after proving their identity. Which policy setup is recommended?",["Conditional Access with user risk condition and block access","Security defaults with risk-based prompts turned on","A named location that excludes risky countries","Conditional Access with sign-in risk condition and require MFA"],3,"A risk-based Conditional Access policy with sign-in risk and MFA lets users self-remediate. Blocking would stop the user completely, and security defaults cannot be customized.","ID Protection",["This uses user risk instead of sign-in risk, and blocking access doesn't let users continue after proving their identity.","Security defaults can't be customized and have no configurable risk-based prompts.","Named locations filter by IP or country; excluding countries doesn't evaluate sign-in risk.",null],3],["sc39",0,2,"A user's risk is high because of leaked credentials. The recommended Conditional Access policy should let the user fix this themselves. What grant should it require?",["Require risk remediation or a secure password change","Require compliant device","Require terms of use acceptance with a new expiry date","Require approved client app"],0,"For user risk, Microsoft recommends requiring a secure password change (or risk remediation) so the risk is cleared. Device compliance does not fix a compromised credential.","ID Protection",[null,"A compliant device doesn't fix a compromised password, so the user risk would remain.","Accepting terms of use doesn't change the leaked password or clear user risk.","Approved client apps control which app is used; they don't remediate a compromised credential."],3],["sc40",0,2,"A user is disabled after they leave, but their access token for Exchange Online is still valid for another 45 minutes. Which feature lets Exchange reject the session quickly?",["Sign-in frequency","Persistent browser session","Continuous access evaluation","Smart lockout"],2,"CAE lets supported services like Exchange Online and SharePoint react in near real time to critical events such as account disable. Sign-in frequency only affects the next authentication.","Conditional Access",["Sign-in frequency only takes effect at the next authentication, so the existing token keeps working until then.","Persistent browser session only controls whether browser sessions survive closing the browser.",null,"Smart lockout blocks repeated failed password attempts; it doesn't revoke existing sessions."],1],["sc41",0,2,"Your company wants to replace its legacy VPN with identity-aware access to internal apps and file shares. Which Microsoft Entra service fits?",["Microsoft Entra Internet Access","Microsoft Entra ID Protection","Microsoft Entra Private Access","Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps discovery"],2,"Private Access, part of Global Secure Access, gives Zero Trust access to private apps and can replace VPNs. Internet Access secures traffic to internet and SaaS destinations.","Global Secure Access",["Internet Access secures traffic to internet and SaaS destinations, not internal apps and file shares.","ID Protection detects identity risk; it doesn't give network access to private apps.",null,"Cloud discovery finds which SaaS apps people use from traffic logs; it doesn't provide access to internal resources."],1],["sc42",0,2,"Global Secure Access should secure only Microsoft 365 traffic at first. What do you turn on?",["The Microsoft traffic forwarding profile","The private access forwarding profile","A named location for Microsoft 365","A Conditional Access policy for the Office 365 app"],0,"Traffic forwarding profiles choose which traffic the Global Secure Access client sends: Microsoft, Internet access or Private access. Named locations do not forward traffic.","Global Secure Access",[null,"The Private Access profile forwards traffic to internal apps, not Microsoft 365.","Named locations describe IP ranges or countries for Conditional Access; they don't forward any traffic.","A Conditional Access policy controls sign-in to Office 365 but doesn't send traffic through Global Secure Access."],2],["sc43",0,2,"Which statement about security defaults and Conditional Access is correct?",["Security defaults apply first, then Conditional Access adds rules","Conditional Access only works when security defaults is enabled","Security defaults can be scoped to exclude named groups","They cannot both be enabled; you must disable security defaults first"],3,"Security defaults must be turned off before you enable Conditional Access policies. Security defaults cannot be scoped or exclude groups.","Security defaults",["They don't layer this way; security defaults have to be disabled before Conditional Access policies can be turned on.","It's the reverse: Conditional Access requires security defaults to be turned off.","Security defaults are all-or-nothing and can't exclude groups or be scoped.",null],1],["sc44",0,2,"Shared kiosk users need to sign in without passwords using a smart card with an X.509 certificate issued by your internal CA. What must you configure?",["Temporary Access Pass for each kiosk user","Certificate-based authentication with the CA uploaded to the tenant","SMS sign-in in the authentication methods policy","Windows Hello for Business cloud Kerberos trust on every shared kiosk device"],1,"Microsoft Entra certificate-based authentication validates user certificates once you upload the issuing CA and enable the method. TAP is temporary, and Windows Hello is tied to one device.","Authentication methods",["A TAP is a temporary code for onboarding or recovery and doesn't use smart card certificates.",null,"SMS sign-in uses a phone number and text message, not an X.509 certificate on a smart card.","Windows Hello for Business is tied to one user on one device, which doesn't suit shared kiosks using smart cards."],2],["sc45",0,2,"Which grant control can you add to Conditional Access to make sure a device is marked compliant by Intune before users access an app?",["Require Microsoft Entra hybrid joined device","Require device to be marked as compliant","Require approved client app","Require authentication strength"],1,"'Require device to be marked as compliant' checks Intune compliance. Hybrid joined only checks join state, not compliance.","Conditional Access",["Hybrid joined only checks that the device is joined to on-premises AD and registered in Entra, not that Intune marks it compliant.",null,"Require approved client app checks the client app used, not device compliance.","Authentication strength controls which sign-in methods count, not the device's compliance state."],1],["sc46",0,2,"An organization wants refresh tokens to be bound to the device they were issued to, so stolen tokens cannot be replayed elsewhere. Which session control addresses this?",["Sign-in frequency","Persistent browser session","Use app enforced restrictions","Token protection"],3,"Token protection binds sign-in session tokens to the device, reducing token theft and replay. The other controls set timing or app behavior.","Conditional Access",["Sign-in frequency sets how often users reauthenticate but doesn't bind tokens to a device.","Persistent browser session controls whether sessions survive closing the browser; stolen tokens could still be replayed.","App enforced restrictions let SharePoint and Exchange limit features such as downloads, not bind tokens to a device.",null],3],["sc47",0,3,"An Azure Function needs to read secrets from Key Vault without any stored credentials. The permission should be deleted with the Function. What should you use?",["A system-assigned managed identity","A user-assigned managed identity","An app registration with a client secret","A service account user with a long password"],0,"A system-assigned managed identity is tied to one resource and deleted with it. A user-assigned identity survives separately, and secrets or service accounts need credential handling.","Managed identities",[null,"A user-assigned identity is a separate resource with its own lifecycle, so it wouldn't be deleted with the Function.","A client secret is a stored credential that has to be protected and rotated, which the requirement rules out.","A service account password is a stored credential and isn't deleted when the Function is removed."],2],["sc48",0,3,"Twenty VMs in a scale set and three App Services all need the same access to a storage account. You want one identity to manage and it should outlive any single resource. What fits best?",["One system-assigned identity per resource","A shared guest account","A user-assigned managed identity","An app registration with a certificate on each VM"],2,"A user-assigned managed identity is a standalone resource that can be attached to many resources and keeps its own lifecycle. System-assigned would mean 23 identities to manage.","Managed identities",["A system-assigned identity per resource means 23 separate identities, each deleted with its own resource.","A shared guest account is a user identity with a password and isn't meant for Azure resources to authenticate.",null,"Certificates on each VM have to be distributed and rotated, and they don't cover the App Services neatly."],2],["sc49",0,3,"A background service with no signed-in user must read all users through Microsoft Graph. Which permission type and consent are needed?",["Delegated permission with user consent","Delegated permission with admin consent","Application permission with admin consent","Application permission with user consent"],2,"Daemon apps use application permissions, which always need admin consent. Delegated permissions need a signed-in user, and users cannot consent to application permissions.","App permissions",["Delegated permissions act on behalf of a signed-in user, and there's no user in a background service.","Delegated permissions still require a signed-in user even when an admin has consented to them.",null,"Users can never consent to application permissions; only an admin can."],1],["sc50",0,3,"Users should be able to consent only to apps from verified publishers asking for low-risk permissions. All other requests should go to reviewers. What do you configure?",["User consent settings plus the admin consent workflow","Assignment required on every enterprise app","A Conditional Access policy for all cloud apps","App roles in each application manifest assigned to all users by default"],0,"User consent settings can allow verified publishers with selected permissions, and the admin consent workflow routes other requests to reviewers. 'Assignment required' controls who can sign in, not consent.","Consent",[null,"Assignment required controls who can sign in to an app, not who can consent to its permissions.","Conditional Access controls how users sign in and has no setting for app consent.","App roles define authorization inside an app; they don't control consent to permissions."],2],["sc51",0,3,"Only members of the HR group should be able to sign in to an enterprise app. What should you do?",["Hide the app from My Apps for other users","Remove the delegated permissions from the app","Create an app role called HR in the manifest","Set Assignment required to Yes and assign the HR group"],3,"With Assignment required on, only assigned users and groups can get tokens for the app. Hiding it in My Apps does not block sign-in, and an app role alone does not restrict access.","Enterprise applications",["Hiding the app from My Apps only removes the tile; users could still sign in with a direct link.","Delegated permissions control what the app can access, not which users can sign in to it.","Defining an app role alone doesn't restrict sign-in unless assignment is required and users are assigned.",null],1],["sc52",0,3,"A developer wants the app to receive a claim showing whether the user is a Reader or an Approver. What should be defined and assigned?",["Directory roles such as Application Administrator","App roles, which appear in the roles claim","Custom security attributes on the app","Optional claims for group names"],1,"App roles are defined on the app registration and assigned to users or groups, and they appear in the token's roles claim. Directory roles are for managing Entra ID, not app authorization.","App roles",["Directory roles such as Application Administrator grant rights to manage Entra ID, not authorization inside the developer's app.",null,"Custom security attributes describe the app for governance and don't appear as a user's role in the token.","Group name claims send group membership, not app-specific roles like Reader or Approver that are defined on the app."],2],["sc53",0,3,"You are setting up SAML SSO for a gallery app. The vendor gives you an Entity ID and an Assertion Consumer Service URL. Where do these go?",["Redirect URI and Application ID URI in app registration","Identifier (Entity ID) and Reply URL in Basic SAML Configuration","Home page URL and Logout URL in Properties","Named location and client app condition in Conditional Access"],1,"The Identifier and Reply URL (ACS) fields in Basic SAML Configuration match what the service provider expects. Redirect URIs are for OAuth/OIDC apps.","SSO",["Redirect URI and Application ID URI belong to OAuth/OIDC app registrations, not SAML configuration of a gallery app.",null,"Home page and logout URLs are app properties for navigation; they don't set the SAML Entity ID or ACS.","Conditional Access settings control sign-in conditions and have nowhere to enter SAML values."],2],["sc54",0,3,"A SAML app stopped working for everyone this morning. The sign-in logs show an error about the signature. What is the most likely cause?",["The Assignment required setting was turned off","A user's license was removed","Seamless SSO was disabled on the domain","The SAML signing certificate expired or was changed"],3,"If the Entra signing certificate expires or is rotated without updating the service provider, assertions fail signature checks for everyone. Turning off assignment would allow more users, not break SSO.","SSO",["Turning off assignment required would let more users sign in; it wouldn't cause signature errors.","Removing one user's license wouldn't break SSO for everyone, and it doesn't affect SAML signatures.","Seamless SSO covers silent sign-in on domain-joined PCs; disabling it doesn't cause SAML signature errors.",null],3],["sc55",0,3,"New hires should get accounts in a SaaS app automatically, and leavers should be disabled there. The app supports SCIM 2.0. What do you configure?",["Automatic user provisioning on the enterprise app","A lifecycle workflow with a custom Logic App only","Cross-tenant synchronization to the vendor tenant","B2B direct connect with the SaaS vendor"],0,"Automatic provisioning uses SCIM to create, update and disable users in the app based on assignments and scoping filters. Cross-tenant sync is only between Entra tenants.","Provisioning",[null,"A lifecycle workflow with only a custom Logic App would be custom work and ignores the app's built-in SCIM support.","Cross-tenant synchronization only works between Microsoft Entra tenants, not a SaaS app's own user store.","B2B direct connect is for Teams shared channels between Entra tenants and doesn't create SaaS accounts."],2],["sc56",0,3,"Where do you check why a specific user was not created in a SaaS app by automatic provisioning?",["The audit logs of Microsoft Entra Connect","The sign-in logs for the app","The provisioning logs","Identity Secure Score"],2,"Provisioning logs show each create, update and skip action with the reason, for example an out-of-scope user. Sign-in logs record authentications, not provisioning.","Provisioning",["Entra Connect audit data covers on-premises sync, not provisioning from Entra ID to SaaS apps.","Sign-in logs record authentications, not whether an account was created in the app.",null,"Identity Secure Score measures security posture and doesn't show provisioning actions."],1],["sc57",0,3,"An internal web app on an on-premises server must be reachable by remote users with Microsoft Entra sign-in and MFA, without opening inbound firewall ports. What should you use?",["A site-to-site VPN and a public IP address on the server","Password hash synchronization with Seamless SSO","Microsoft Entra application proxy with a private network connector","B2B direct connect with the on-premises domain"],2,"Application proxy connectors make outbound connections only, and users authenticate with Entra ID and Conditional Access. A public IP would need inbound ports.","Application proxy",["A public IP address needs inbound firewall ports, which the requirement rules out.","PHS with Seamless SSO handles authentication but doesn't publish an internal web app to remote users.",null,"B2B direct connect is for Teams shared channels between tenants and doesn't publish on-premises apps."],2],["sc58",0,3,"You want to find which unsanctioned cloud apps employees are using, based on firewall logs. Which feature helps?",["Cloud discovery in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps","Access reviews for enterprise apps","Microsoft Entra Connect Health with the AD FS agent on each proxy","Admin consent requests"],0,"Cloud discovery analyzes traffic logs to find shadow IT apps and give risk scores. Access reviews only cover apps already integrated with Entra ID.","Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"Access reviews only cover apps already integrated with Entra ID, not unsanctioned apps found in traffic.","The AD FS agent monitors federation servers and doesn't analyze firewall logs for app usage.","Admin consent requests only show apps users tried to consent to, not all SaaS traffic from firewall logs."],1],["sc59",0,3,"A single-tenant service principal used by a CI pipeline should only be able to get tokens from your build agents' public IP range. What can enforce this?",["A named location assigned to the app registration","Assignment required on the service principal","A user risk policy targeting service principals","Conditional Access for workload identities with a location condition"],3,"Conditional Access for workload identities (Workload Identities Premium) can block service principals outside given locations. Named locations alone do nothing unless used in a policy.","Workload identities",["Named locations can't be assigned to app registrations directly; they only take effect inside a Conditional Access policy.","Assignment required controls which users can sign in to an app, not where a service principal gets tokens from.","User risk policies apply to users, not service principals, and don't restrict by IP range.",null],3],["sc60",0,3,"A GitHub Actions workflow deploys to Azure. Security wants no long-lived secrets stored in GitHub. What should the app registration use?",["A client secret with a 24-month expiry","A federated identity credential for GitHub","A certificate stored as a GitHub secret","A user account with MFA excluded"],1,"Workload identity federation trusts tokens from GitHub's OIDC issuer, so no secret is stored. Certificates and secrets are still long-lived credentials that can leak.","Workload identities",["A client secret is exactly the kind of long-lived secret stored in GitHub that security wants to avoid.",null,"A certificate stored as a GitHub secret is still a long-lived credential that could leak.","A user account with MFA excluded is insecure and still needs a stored password."],2],["sc61",0,3,"Which statement about application objects and service principals is correct?",["Each tenant using a multitenant app gets its own copy of the application object","The app object is the global definition; a service principal is its local instance in each tenant","Service principals exist only for managed identities, not for apps","Deleting a service principal also deletes the app object in the home tenant"],1,"The application object lives in the home tenant; each tenant that uses the app gets a service principal. Deleting a service principal does not remove the home app object.","Workload identities",["It's the reverse: other tenants get a service principal, while the single application object stays in the home tenant.",null,"Every app used in a tenant has a service principal, not just managed identities.","Deleting a service principal removes only that tenant's local instance; the app object in the home tenant remains."],3],["sc62",0,3,"Security wants to find app registrations whose client secrets expire within 30 days, and apps that have not signed in for months. Where should you start?",["Entitlement management catalog reports","The Conditional Access insights and reporting workbook for applications","Password Protection audit logs on DCs","Microsoft Entra recommendations and the app registrations credentials view"],3,"Entra recommendations flag expiring credentials and unused apps, and the app registrations list shows certificates and secrets with expiry dates. The other tools do not track app credentials.","Workload identities",["Entitlement management reports cover access package assignments, not app credentials or app sign-in activity.","The Conditional Access insights workbook shows policy results, not credential expiry dates.","Password Protection logs on DCs record password changes checked against banned lists, not app registrations.",null],2],["sc63",0,3,"Users on unmanaged devices should be able to open Salesforce, but downloads of files labeled Confidential must be blocked in real time. What should you configure?",["A Conditional Access App Control session policy in Defender for Cloud Apps","An app role that removes download rights","An access review of Salesforce users","The Salesforce provisioning scoping filter based on the Confidential label"],0,"Routing the session through Conditional Access App Control lets Defender for Cloud Apps session policies block downloads live. App roles and provisioning do not inspect sessions.","Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"App roles are claims the app interprets; they can't block downloads of labeled files in real time.","An access review confirms who needs access; it doesn't control downloads during a session.","Provisioning scoping filters decide which users get accounts, and they don't inspect file labels."],3],["sc64",0,3,"A new app needs Mail.Send delegated permission. Users see 'Need admin approval' and cannot continue. What is the best way to let them request access?",["Grant Global Administrator to the requesting users","Turn on 'Users can register applications'","Enable the admin consent workflow and name reviewers","Set the app to multitenant"],2,"The admin consent workflow lets users submit a request that designated reviewers approve or deny. Granting admin roles to users breaks least privilege.","Consent",["Making users Global Administrators breaks least privilege badly just to grant one app permission.","That setting lets users create app registrations; it doesn't let them get admin consent for this app.",null,"Making the app multitenant changes who can use it, not how admin-approved permissions are requested."],2],["sc65",0,3,"ID Protection shows a service principal with high risk because its credentials appeared in a public code repository. What should you do first?",["Add the service principal to an access review","Exclude it from all Conditional Access policies","Remove the leaked credential and add a new one","Change its owner to another user"],2,"A leaked credential should be removed and replaced right away, then you investigate activity. An access review or new owner would not stop use of the leaked secret.","Workload identities",["An access review is slow and doesn't stop anyone from using the leaked credential right now.","Excluding it from Conditional Access would reduce protection for a compromised identity.",null,"Changing the owner doesn't invalidate the leaked credential, which still works."],2],["sc66",0,3,"A web app signs users in and calls an API on their behalf. It is built on OpenID Connect. Which kind of SSO do you configure for it in Microsoft Entra ID?",["OIDC/OAuth through the app registration","Password-based SSO in My Apps","Linked SSO to an external URL","Header-based SSO through a SAML proxy"],0,"Modern apps that use OpenID Connect are configured through app registration with redirect URIs and permissions. Password-based SSO stores credentials for legacy apps.","SSO",[null,"Password-based SSO stores and replays credentials for legacy apps that can't use modern protocols.","Linked SSO only adds a link in My Apps and doesn't sign the user in with OIDC.","Header-based SSO is for legacy on-premises apps that read identity from HTTP headers, not OIDC apps."],2],["sc67",0,3,"The finance team wants a list of which users signed in to each enterprise app in the last 30 days, to spot unused apps. Where do you look?",["Provisioning logs","Audit logs filtered by the consent and app role assignment categories","Entitlement management access package reports","Sign-in logs, or the application usage workbook"],3,"Sign-in logs (and workbooks built on them) show app sign-in activity. Audit logs record changes, not sign-ins.","Monitoring apps",["Provisioning logs show account create and update actions in apps, not user sign-ins.","Audit logs record configuration changes like consents and role assignments, not sign-in activity.","Access package reports show who was assigned packages, not who actually signed in to each app.",null],2],["sc68",0,4,"Contractors should get a bundle of a Teams team, a SharePoint site and an app for 90 days after their manager approves. Which feature fits?",["A dynamic group with an expiry date","An access package in entitlement management","A PIM for Groups eligible assignment","A lifecycle workflow mover template"],1,"Access packages bundle resources with request, approval and expiration policies. Dynamic groups have no approval or expiry.","Entitlement management",["Dynamic groups add members based on attributes and have no manager approval or built-in expiry.",null,"PIM for Groups gives just-in-time group membership, not a request-and-approval bundle of a team, site and app for contractors.","A mover workflow reacts to attribute changes for existing employees; it doesn't handle approved requests for contractors."],2],["sc69",0,4,"Users from a partner organization with their own Microsoft Entra tenant should be able to request an access package themselves. What must you set up?",["A B2B direct connect trust with the partner","A connected organization for the partner","Cross-tenant synchronization from the partner","An administrative unit for partner users"],1,"Connected organizations let users in that external directory or domain request access packages, and they are added as guests on approval. Cross-tenant sync is for tenants in the same organization.","Entitlement management",["B2B direct connect is for Teams shared channels and doesn't let partners request access packages.",null,"Cross-tenant synchronization is for tenants in the same organization and pushes users rather than letting partners request access.","Administrative units scope admin roles and have nothing to do with external users requesting access."],2],["sc70",0,4,"Users who have the Payment Approver access package must not also get the Payment Creator package. What should you configure?",["A Conditional Access block policy","Different catalogs for the two packages","Access review auto-apply with deny","Incompatible access packages (separation of duties)"],3,"Entitlement management separation of duties lets you mark access packages or groups as incompatible so a user cannot request both. Separate catalogs do not prevent that.","Entitlement management",["Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions; it can't prevent a user from being assigned a second access package.","Catalogs organize resources and delegation; a user could still request packages from both catalogs.","An access review removes access periodically after the fact; it doesn't stop the second package being assigned.",null],3],["sc71",0,4,"Each quarter, group owners must confirm whether guest members still need access, and access should be removed if an owner does not respond. What do you create?",["A recurring access review with auto-apply and 'remove access' if no response","A PIM alert for stale assignments","A lifecycle workflow leaver template","A Conditional Access policy for guests that blocks sign-ins after 90 days of inactivity"],0,"Recurring access reviews can target guests in groups, use owners as reviewers, and auto-apply removal when reviewers don't respond. PIM alerts concern role assignments.","Access reviews",[null,"PIM alerts cover privileged role settings and assignments, not guest membership in groups.","A leaver workflow runs when an employee leaves; it doesn't ask owners to confirm guests every quarter.","Conditional Access can't block sign-ins based on 90 days of inactivity, and it doesn't involve owners or remove membership."],2],["sc72",0,4,"Reviewers want help deciding in an access review. Which built-in recommendation is based on activity?",["Approve all users who have a manager attribute that matches the group owner","Deny for users without a license","Deny for users who have not signed in within the inactivity period","Approve users who registered MFA"],2,"Access reviews recommend denying users who have been inactive (no sign-in) for the set period, and can also use user-to-group affiliation. License and MFA state are not recommendation inputs.","Access reviews",["Manager-to-owner matching isn't a recommendation input; the affiliation recommendation compares users to the group's other members.","License state isn't used to generate access review recommendations.",null,"MFA registration isn't a recommendation input and says nothing about whether the user still needs access."],2],["sc73",0,4,"The security team wants helpdesk staff to hold User Administrator only when needed, for up to 2 hours, with MFA and a justification. What should you use?",["A permanent active assignment scoped to an administrative unit","A custom role with fewer permissions","An eligible assignment in PIM with activation settings","An access package that includes the role for 2 hours"],2,"PIM eligible assignments require activation, and role settings set maximum duration, MFA and justification. Active assignments give standing access.","PIM",["A permanent active assignment is standing access, even when scoped to an administrative unit.","A custom role reduces permissions but still gives standing access with no activation, time limit or justification.",null,"Access packages grant assignments through requests and approvals; they don't give eligible roles with on-demand 2-hour activation, MFA and justification the way PIM does."],2],["sc74",0,4,"Activating Global Administrator in PIM must be approved by two security leads. Where is this set?",["The role settings for Global Administrator in PIM","The Conditional Access policy for Directory roles","The access package policy for admin roles","The administrative unit that contains the admins"],0,"PIM role settings define approval and approvers for activation. Conditional Access controls sign-in, not role activation approval.","PIM",[null,"Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions and doesn't approve PIM role activations.","Access package policies govern access package requests, not PIM activation of Global Administrator.","Administrative units scope roles to sets of objects and have no activation approval settings."],2],["sc75",0,4,"Admins need just-in-time membership in a security group that grants access to sensitive Azure resources. Which feature covers this?",["A dynamic group with an admin rule","Access reviews of the group","Group-based licensing","PIM for Groups"],3,"PIM for Groups allows eligible membership or ownership with time-limited activation. Dynamic groups and access reviews do not give just-in-time membership.","PIM",["A dynamic group adds members from attributes and gives permanent membership, not time-limited activation.","Access reviews check existing membership periodically; they don't give just-in-time membership.","Group-based licensing assigns licenses to members and doesn't grant just-in-time membership.",null],1],["sc76",0,4,"A PIM alert says there are too many Global Administrators. What is the recommended fix?",["Delete the alert rule because Global Administrators are required","Convert most to eligible or lower roles, keeping a small number plus break-glass accounts","Add all Global Administrators to an administrative unit","Exclude them from MFA and require compliant devices to reduce support calls and lockouts"],1,"Microsoft recommends fewer than five Global Administrators, using least-privileged roles and eligible assignments. Administrative units do not scope Global Administrator.","PIM",["Deleting the alert hides the problem instead of reducing standing Global Administrator access.",null,"Administrative units can't scope Global Administrator, which is always tenant-wide.","Excluding admins from MFA would make the riskiest accounts much weaker."],3],["sc77",0,4,"New hires should receive a Temporary Access Pass and group memberships automatically before their first day, based on the employeeHireDate attribute. What should you use?",["An access review scoped to new users","A lifecycle workflow using the onboarding template","A dynamic group with a hire-date rule and SSPR","A PIM eligible assignment that starts on hire date"],1,"Lifecycle workflows run joiner tasks, such as generating a TAP and adding groups, triggered by employeeHireDate. Dynamic groups cannot issue a TAP.","Lifecycle workflows",["Access reviews confirm existing access periodically and can't issue a TAP before day one.",null,"Dynamic groups can add memberships but can't generate a Temporary Access Pass, and SSPR needs registered methods.","PIM eligible assignments cover privileged roles and groups, not TAP generation and onboarding tasks."],2],["sc78",0,4,"Which license is needed for lifecycle workflows?",["Microsoft Entra ID P1 only","Microsoft Entra ID Free","Workload Identities Premium","Microsoft Entra ID Governance"],3,"Lifecycle workflows are a Microsoft Entra ID Governance feature. P1 covers features such as Conditional Access and dynamic groups.","Licensing",["P1 includes features such as Conditional Access and dynamic groups, but not lifecycle workflows.","The Free tier doesn't include any identity governance automation like lifecycle workflows.","Workload Identities Premium covers features for service principals, not user lifecycle workflows.",null],1],["sc79",0,4,"Guests must accept a data-handling agreement before they can open any company app, and accept it again every year. What should you use?",["Terms of use with a Conditional Access grant control","A custom banner in company branding","An access package with a questionnaire","An access review that guests complete themselves"],0,"Terms of use can be required through Conditional Access and set to expire so users accept again. Branding only shows text and does not enforce acceptance.","Terms of use",[null,"Company branding only changes the sign-in page look and text; it can't force acceptance or re-acceptance.","An access package questionnaire is answered when requesting a package, not before opening every app each year.","Access reviews confirm whether access is still needed; they don't make users accept an agreement."],2],["sc80",0,4,"Your tenant has P1 licenses. An investigation needs sign-in logs from 60 days ago, but they are gone. What should have been in place?",["A longer smart lockout duration","Identity Secure Score history export","A diagnostic setting sending logs to Log Analytics or storage","Security defaults with extended audit mode and a 90-day log retention setting"],2,"With P1/P2, sign-in logs stay 30 days in Entra ID. Diagnostic settings send them to Log Analytics, storage or Event Hubs for longer retention.","Monitoring",["Smart lockout duration controls how long accounts stay locked after failed attempts, not log retention.","Secure Score history shows posture over time, not individual sign-in log entries.",null,"Security defaults have no extended audit mode or retention setting."],3],["sc81",0,4,"How long are sign-in and audit logs kept in a tenant with only Microsoft Entra ID Free?",["30 days","90 days","7 days","1 year"],2,"Free tenants keep these logs for 7 days, and P1/P2 tenants for 30 days. For longer, export them with diagnostic settings.","Monitoring",["30 days is the retention for tenants with P1 or P2 licenses, not Free tenants.","90 days is longer than Entra ID keeps sign-in and audit logs at any license level.",null,"Entra ID doesn't keep sign-in and audit logs for a year; you'd need to export them."],1],["sc82",0,4,"The SOC uses a third-party SIEM and wants Entra sign-in and audit logs streamed in near real time. Which destination should the diagnostic setting use?",["Azure Event Hubs","A storage account archive","Identity Secure Score","A Microsoft Entra workbook"],0,"Event Hubs streams logs to external SIEMs. Storage accounts are for low-cost archive, not streaming.","Monitoring",[null,"A storage account archives logs cheaply for later, but it isn't a near real-time stream to a SIEM.","Identity Secure Score is a posture score and can't be a diagnostic setting destination.","Workbooks visualize logs already in Log Analytics; they aren't a destination for streaming logs."],1],["sc83",0,4,"Who changed a Conditional Access policy yesterday? Where do you find this?",["Sign-in logs","Provisioning logs","Usage and insights for app activity","Audit logs"],3,"Audit logs record changes to directory objects and policies, including who made them. Sign-in logs record authentications.","Monitoring",["Sign-in logs record authentications, not who changed a policy.","Provisioning logs record accounts created or updated in apps, not policy changes.","Usage and insights show app sign-in activity, not changes to Conditional Access policies.",null],1],["sc84",0,4,"Management wants a single score of how well identity security best practices are adopted, with a list of improvement actions. What should you show them?",["The Conditional Access insights workbook","Identity Secure Score","PIM alerts","Access review history report"],1,"Identity Secure Score measures adoption of recommended identity settings and lists improvement actions. The workbook focuses on Conditional Access results only.","Monitoring",["The Conditional Access insights workbook only shows how Conditional Access policies affect sign-ins; it isn't an overall score.",null,"PIM alerts flag specific privileged access issues rather than giving one overall score.","Access review history reports show review decisions, not overall adoption of best practices."],1],["sc85",0,4,"You send SignInLogs to Log Analytics. Which query finds failed sign-ins over the last day?",["AuditLogs | where OperationName == \"Sign-in\" and Result == \"success\"","SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1d) | where ResultType != \"0\"","SigninLogs | where ResultType == \"0\" | summarize count()","Heartbeat | where Computer contains \"login\""],1,"In SigninLogs, ResultType \"0\" means success, so a filter on non-zero values returns failures. AuditLogs holds directory changes, not sign-ins.","Monitoring",["AuditLogs holds directory changes, not sign-ins, and this filter looks for successes, not failures.",null,"ResultType 0 means success, so this counts successful sign-ins and has no time filter.","The Heartbeat table holds agent health data, not user sign-ins."],2],["sc86",0,4,"Access package assignments for contractors should end after 6 months unless extended, and managers must review them every quarter. Where is this set?",["The catalog's owner settings","A PIM role setting for the catalog","The contractors' administrative unit with a restricted management setting enabled","The access package's assignment policy (expiration and access reviews)"],3,"The assignment policy in an access package sets expiration and recurring access reviews. Catalogs hold resources and delegated owners but not these lifecycle settings.","Entitlement management",["Catalog owner settings delegate who manages the catalog; they don't set assignment expiry or reviews.","PIM role settings apply to privileged roles and groups, not catalogs or access package assignments.","A restricted management administrative unit limits which admins can change users; it doesn't expire access.",null],3],["sc87",0,4,"When a user moves from Sales to Marketing, their Sales group memberships should be removed and Marketing ones added automatically. Which approach fits best?",["A mover lifecycle workflow or dynamic groups based on department","A quarterly access review of all department groups, with owners as reviewers","A PIM for Groups assignment per department","A terms of use policy for each department"],0,"Mover workflows (or attribute-based dynamic groups) change access when the department changes. Quarterly reviews would leave excess access for months.","Lifecycle workflows",[null,"A quarterly review leaves the old Sales access in place for up to three months and doesn't add Marketing groups.","PIM for Groups gives just-in-time activation, not automatic changes when the department attribute changes.","Terms of use makes users accept an agreement and doesn't change group memberships."],3],["sc88",0,4,"Which license do you need to use Privileged Identity Management for Microsoft Entra roles?",["Microsoft Entra ID P1","Microsoft Entra ID Free","Microsoft Entra ID P2 or Microsoft Entra ID Governance","Microsoft 365 Business Basic with the Entra ID add-on for administrators"],2,"PIM needs Entra ID P2 or Entra ID Governance licenses for the users who benefit from it. P1 does not include PIM.","Licensing",["P1 covers Conditional Access and dynamic groups, but PIM needs P2 or Entra ID Governance.","The Free tier doesn't include Privileged Identity Management.",null,"Business Basic includes only Entra ID Free features, and PIM needs P2 or Entra ID Governance licenses, not a generic admin add-on."],1],["x1",0,1,"Non-admin users are registering their own applications in the company tenant. Administrators want to stop regular users from creating app registrations. Where is this controlled?",["Cross-tenant access settings, default outbound","External collaboration settings for guest invitations","User settings: 'Users can register applications' set to No","Company branding, with a notice on the sign-in page telling users not to register apps"],2,"The 'Users can register applications' user setting controls whether non-admin users can create app registrations. External collaboration settings govern guests and invitations, not app registration by members.","Domain 1: Tenant user settings",["Outbound cross-tenant settings govern your users accessing other tenants, not app registration inside yours.","External collaboration settings control guest invitations and guest access, not members registering apps.",null,"Branding only changes how the sign-in page looks; a notice cannot technically stop app registration."],2],["x2",0,1,"You want to assign the Helpdesk Administrator role to a group so that adding or removing members grants or removes the role. What must be true of that group?",["It was created with 'Microsoft Entra roles can be assigned to the group' enabled","It uses dynamic membership based on each user's job title","It is a Microsoft 365 group that has a connected team","It is synchronized from on-premises Active Directory and has at least one owner assigned"],0,"Only role-assignable groups, which must have that option set when the group is created, can receive Microsoft Entra role assignments. Dynamic membership is not allowed for role-assignable groups, because rules could grant admin roles unintentionally.","Domain 1: Groups and role assignment",[null,"Role-assignable groups cannot use dynamic membership, because rules could grant admin roles unintentionally.","Being a Microsoft 365 group with a team is irrelevant; the role-assignable option is what matters.","Syncing from AD and having owners does not make a group role-assignable, and synced groups cannot be made so."],3],["x3",0,1,"All Windows devices whose name starts with 'KIOSK-' should be added automatically to a group used for policy targeting. What should be created?",["A dynamic user group with a rule on user.department","An administrative unit with assigned membership","A Microsoft 365 group whose owners approve each device join request manually","A dynamic device group with a rule on device.displayName"],3,"Dynamic device groups evaluate device attributes such as displayName and add matching devices automatically. A dynamic user group evaluates user attributes, so it cannot select devices by name.","Domain 1: Dynamic groups",["A dynamic user group evaluates user attributes, so it cannot select devices by their names.","Assigned membership in an administrative unit is manual and does not add devices automatically.","Microsoft 365 groups contain users, not devices, and manual approval is not automatic membership.",null],2],["x4",0,1,"Employees want to read company email on personal phones. The company wants those devices known to Microsoft Entra ID for Conditional Access, without taking organizational ownership of them. Which device identity fits?",["Microsoft Entra joined","Microsoft Entra registered","Microsoft Entra hybrid joined","Domain joined over a VPN"],1,"Microsoft Entra registered is intended for personal (BYOD) devices, which are signed in with a work account but remain personally owned. Microsoft Entra join is for organization-owned devices that sign in with an organizational account.","Domain 1: Device identities",["Microsoft Entra join is for organization-owned devices, not employees' personal phones.",null,"Hybrid join requires the device to be joined to on-premises AD, which personal phones are not.","Domain join over VPN is an on-premises concept and does not give Entra ID a device identity for phones."],1],["x5",0,1,"Only members of the IT group should be able to join Windows devices to Microsoft Entra ID. What should be configured?",["A dynamic device group based on the IT department","Device settings: 'Users may join devices to Microsoft Entra' set to Selected","The 'Users can register applications' user setting, limited to members of the IT group","Enterprise State Roaming limited to the IT group"],1,"The 'Users may join devices to Microsoft Entra' device setting can be limited to selected users or groups. A dynamic device group only groups devices after they exist; it does not control who may join them.","Domain 1: Device settings",["A dynamic device group groups devices after they exist; it cannot control who is allowed to join them.",null,"That setting controls app registrations, not joining devices, whatever group it is limited to.","Enterprise State Roaming syncs user settings across devices and does not govern who can join them."],2],["x6",0,1,"A guest redeemed an invitation using the wrong personal account. The admin wants the guest to redeem again with the correct account while keeping existing group memberships and app assignments. What should be done?",["Delete the guest user and send a new invitation","Change the guest's user principal name in the portal","Add the guest's domain as a connected organization","Reset the guest's redemption status"],3,"Resetting redemption status lets the guest redeem again with a different account while the same user object, and therefore its memberships and assignments, is kept. Deleting and reinviting creates a new object and loses all existing access.","Domain 1: B2B collaboration",["Deleting and reinviting creates a new user object, losing all existing memberships and assignments.","Changing the UPN does not change which external account redeemed the invitation.","Connected organizations are for entitlement management requests, not fixing a guest's redemption.",null],3],["x7",0,1,"Security wants to stop internal users from being invited into, and signing in to, other organizations' tenants as guests, except for one approved partner. What should be configured?",["Block outbound B2B collaboration by default, and allow the partner org","Block inbound B2B collaboration in the default settings for all tenants","Restrict guest invitations to the Guest Inviter role only","Set guest user access to the most restrictive level"],0,"Outbound cross-tenant access settings control whether your users can access other organizations, and an organizational setting can allow one partner. Inbound settings control external users coming into your tenant, the opposite direction.","Domain 1: Cross-tenant access settings",[null,"Inbound settings control external users coming into your tenant, the opposite direction.","Restricting who can invite guests affects invitations into your tenant, not your users going elsewhere.","Guest user access levels limit what guests see in your directory, not where your users can go."],3],["x8",0,1,"Users want to work with a partner company in Teams shared channels without creating guest accounts in either tenant. What should be enabled?",["B2B collaboration guest invitations","Cross-tenant synchronization","B2B direct connect in both tenants","Microsoft Entra External ID for customers"],2,"B2B direct connect is a mutual trust that lets users from both organizations collaborate in Teams shared channels without guest objects. B2B collaboration creates guest users in the resource tenant, which is what they want to avoid.","Domain 1: B2B direct connect",["B2B collaboration creates guest user objects in the resource tenant, which the users want to avoid.","Cross-tenant synchronization creates B2B users automatically, which still means guest accounts.",null,"External ID for customers is for consumer-facing apps, not Teams collaboration with a partner company."],1],["x9",0,1,"Only users in the Employees OU should synchronize to Microsoft Entra ID; service accounts in other OUs must never appear in the cloud. What should be configured?",["A dynamic group that excludes service accounts","A scoping filter on cross-tenant synchronization","A Conditional Access policy that blocks service accounts","Domain and OU filtering in Microsoft Entra Connect"],3,"Domain and OU-based filtering in Microsoft Entra Connect limits which on-premises objects are synchronized. A Conditional Access policy would still let the service accounts sync into the tenant, only blocking their sign-ins.","Domain 1: Hybrid identity sync",["A dynamic group can only group accounts that have already synced into the cloud.","Cross-tenant synchronization copies users between Entra tenants, not from on-premises AD.","A Conditional Access block still leaves the service accounts synced into the cloud directory.",null],2],["x10",0,1,"The company wants a second Microsoft Entra Connect Sync server ready to take over if the primary fails. It should import and synchronize but not export changes. How should it be deployed?",["As a second active server exporting in parallel","As a Microsoft Entra Connect Health agent","As a server in staging mode","As a Cloud Sync agent on the same server"],2,"A staging mode server runs import and sync but does not export, so it can be switched to active if the primary fails. Two active Connect Sync servers exporting to the same tenant is not a supported configuration.","Domain 1: Microsoft Entra Connect Sync",["Two active Connect Sync servers exporting to the same tenant is not supported and causes conflicts.","Connect Health monitors sync; it cannot take over synchronization if the primary fails.",null,"A Cloud Sync agent is a different sync engine and is not a standby copy of the Connect Sync server."],2],["x11",0,1,"A company uses pass-through authentication with only one authentication agent installed. What should be done to avoid sign-in outages if that server goes down?",["Enable Seamless SSO for domain-joined devices","Install additional PTA agents on other servers","Switch Entra Connect Sync into staging mode","Lengthen the synchronization interval"],1,"Additional pass-through authentication agents on other servers provide high availability for sign-in requests. Seamless SSO only removes password prompts on corporate devices and still relies on a working authentication method.","Domain 1: Pass-through authentication",["Seamless SSO removes password prompts on corporate devices but still depends on a working PTA agent.",null,"Staging mode concerns the sync server, not the availability of authentication agents.","The sync interval affects how often changes sync, not whether sign-ins can be validated."],2],["x12",0,1,"Federated sign-ins through AD FS fail intermittently. Admins want alerts, performance data and reports of failed sign-ins for the AD FS servers. What should they use?",["Microsoft Entra Connect Health for AD FS","Identity Secure Score improvement actions","Privileged Identity Management alerts","Provisioning logs for enterprise apps"],0,"Connect Health for AD FS monitors the federation servers, raising alerts and showing usage, performance and failed sign-in data. Identity Secure Score measures configuration posture and does not monitor AD FS servers.","Domain 1: Microsoft Entra Connect Health",[null,"Identity Secure Score measures configuration posture and does not monitor AD FS server health.","PIM alerts cover privileged role configuration, not federation server failures.","Provisioning logs track account provisioning to SaaS apps, not AD FS sign-in failures."],2],["x13",0,1,"A synchronization error reports that two on-premises users have the same proxyAddresses value. What is the correct fix?",["Fix the duplicate in on-premises AD, then let sync run","Edit the synced attribute directly on the cloud user","Reinstall Entra Connect so it rebuilds its metaverse","Turn off duplicate attribute resiliency in the tenant"],0,"On-premises AD is the source of authority for synced users, so the duplicate must be corrected there and the next sync cycle clears the error. Synced attributes cannot be edited on the cloud object.","Domain 1: Troubleshooting sync errors",[null,"Synced attributes are mastered on-premises and cannot be edited on the cloud object.","Reinstalling does not remove the duplicate value from AD, so the error returns.","Duplicate attribute resiliency is always on and cannot be turned off; it is not the fix anyway."],2],["x14",0,1,"Users should see the company logo, a background image and a helpdesk message on the Microsoft Entra sign-in page. What should be configured?",["Custom domain names","Company branding","Terms of use","A registration campaign"],1,"Company branding customizes the sign-in experience with logos, background images and text. Custom domain names change users' sign-in names but not what the sign-in page looks like.","Domain 1: Tenant setup",["Custom domain names change users' sign-in suffix, not the look of the sign-in page.",null,"Terms of use present a document to accept; they do not add logos or backgrounds to the sign-in page.","Registration campaigns nudge users to set up Authenticator, not customize the sign-in page."],1],["x15",0,2,"Users keep approving Microsoft Authenticator push requests they did not start (MFA fatigue). Which Authenticator settings help most?",["Allow SMS as a fallback method for everyone","Shorten the sign-in frequency to one hour","Number matching with app name and location context","Turn off combined security info registration so users cannot add new methods"],2,"Number matching makes the user type the number shown on the sign-in screen, and additional context shows which app and location made the request, so blind approvals fail. SMS is a weaker method and adds no protection against fatigue.","Domain 2: Microsoft Authenticator",["SMS is weaker and adds no protection against users approving prompts they did not start.","More frequent sign-ins generate more prompts, which can make fatigue worse.",null,"Blocking method registration does nothing to stop users approving push requests they did not start."],2],["x16",0,2,"Administrators must sign in only with a specific model of company-issued FIDO2 security key, not with personal keys. What should the Conditional Access policy require?",["The built-in phishing-resistant MFA strength","A Temporary Access Pass on every sign-in","A device marked as compliant by Intune, with a security key registered to the user in the portal","A custom authentication strength that allows specific FIDO2 key AAGUIDs"],3,"Custom authentication strengths can restrict FIDO2 security keys to allowed AAGUIDs, which identify the key model. The built-in phishing-resistant strength accepts any FIDO2 key, including personal ones.","Domain 2: Authentication strengths",["The built-in phishing-resistant strength accepts any FIDO2 key, including personal ones.","A Temporary Access Pass is for onboarding or recovery, and it is not phishing-resistant for everyday use.","Device compliance checks the device, not which security key model is used to sign in.",null],3],["x17",0,2,"SSPR is configured to require one method for users, yet administrators find they must supply two methods to reset their own passwords. Why?",["Admin roles are excluded from SSPR altogether","Security defaults override SSPR for all admins","Microsoft applies a separate, stronger reset policy to admins","Admins must have password writeback to reset"],2,"Microsoft enforces a default administrator reset policy requiring two gates, regardless of the user SSPR settings. Admins are not excluded from SSPR; they simply follow the stricter policy.","Domain 2: Self-service password reset",["Admins are not excluded; they can use SSPR but follow a stricter built-in policy.","Security defaults do not override SSPR method counts for admins.",null,"Password writeback syncs resets to on-premises AD and has nothing to do with the number of methods."],3],["x18",0,2,"A tenant uses pass-through authentication and smart lockout. How should the AD DS account lockout threshold relate to the Microsoft Entra lockout threshold so attackers are locked out in the cloud before AD accounts lock?",["Set the AD DS threshold well above the Entra threshold","Set both thresholds to exactly the same value","Set the Entra threshold above the AD DS threshold","Disable AD DS lockout when smart lockout is on"],0,"Microsoft guidance is to keep the Entra lockout threshold lower than AD DS, with AD DS several times higher, so smart lockout stops attacks before on-premises accounts lock. Equal thresholds let attacks lock AD accounts at the same moment.","Domain 2: Smart lockout",[null,"Equal thresholds let an attack lock the on-premises account at the same moment as the cloud one.","A higher Entra threshold means AD accounts lock before smart lockout can protect them.","Disabling AD lockout removes on-premises protection entirely and is not the guidance."],3],["x19",0,2,"A Conditional Access policy must block only legacy authentication, such as IMAP and POP with basic authentication. Which condition selects those sign-ins?",["Device platforms","Sign-in risk","Filter for devices on the operating system version attribute","Client apps: Exchange ActiveSync and other clients"],3,"The client apps condition includes 'Exchange ActiveSync clients' and 'Other clients', which represent legacy authentication. Device platforms describe the OS, not the authentication protocol.","Domain 2: Conditional Access conditions",["Device platforms describe the operating system, not the authentication protocol being used.","Sign-in risk reflects the likelihood of compromise, not whether legacy protocols are used.","Device filters use device attributes, and legacy clients often send no device information.",null],2],["x20",0,2,"With continuous access evaluation, security wants resources such as Exchange Online to reject a token immediately when the user's IP moves outside the allowed named locations. What should be enabled?",["Sign-in frequency set to every hour","Strict location enforcement for CAE","Never-persistent browser sessions","A shorter access token lifetime policy"],1,"Strict location enforcement makes CAE-capable resources evaluate the client IP against location policies and reject tokens used from disallowed IPs. Sign-in frequency only forces re-authentication at set intervals, not on an IP change.","Domain 2: Continuous access evaluation",["Sign-in frequency forces re-authentication on a schedule, not immediately when the IP changes.",null,"Browser session persistence affects cookies after closing the browser, not IP enforcement.","Shorter token lifetimes still leave a window and are not how CAE enforces location."],3],["x21",0,2,"A user's laptop is stolen. The admin resets the password and also wants all of the user's refresh tokens invalidated immediately. What should be done?",["Disable MFA for the account","Revoke the user's sessions","Remove the user's licenses","Delete a registered phone number"],1,"Revoking sessions invalidates the user's refresh tokens and session cookies so they must sign in again. Removing licenses stops service access but does not invalidate tokens for sign-in.","Domain 2: Session revocation",["Disabling MFA weakens the account and does nothing to invalidate issued tokens.",null,"Removing licenses stops service entitlement but does not invalidate refresh tokens.","Deleting one phone number removes a method but leaves existing sessions valid."],1],["x22",0,2,"A Conditional Access policy should block access from devices running Linux. Which condition should be used?",["Client apps","Named locations","Filter for devices on trustType and the device ownership attribute","Device platforms, with Linux selected"],3,"The device platforms condition lets a policy target operating systems, including Linux. Client apps targets the type of client or protocol, not the OS.","Domain 2: Conditional Access conditions",["Client apps targets browser, mobile apps or legacy clients, not the operating system.","Named locations are IP or country based and say nothing about the device's OS.","trustType and ownership describe how a device is joined or owned, not that it runs Linux.",null],1],["x23",0,2,"Guest users from any external organization must complete MFA to access company apps; employees are covered by another policy. What should be created?",["A Conditional Access policy for guest or external users, requiring MFA","An MFA requirement in external collaboration settings","An MFA requirement in cross-tenant outbound settings","Security defaults scoped only to guest accounts"],0,"Conditional Access can target 'Guest or external users' and require MFA for them. Outbound settings apply to your own users going to other tenants, not guests coming in.","Domain 2: Conditional Access for guests",[null,"External collaboration settings control invitations and guest access, not MFA requirements.","Outbound settings apply to your own users going to other tenants, not guests coming in.","Security defaults cannot be scoped to specific users such as guests."],2],["x24",0,2,"A user was blocked from an app this morning. You want to see which Conditional Access policies applied to that actual sign-in and which grant control failed. Where should you look?",["Audit logs filtered to the Conditional Access category","Provisioning logs for the affected enterprise app","The sign-in log entry's Conditional Access details","The Identity Secure Score improvement actions list"],2,"Each sign-in log entry records the Conditional Access policies evaluated, their result and the controls that were not satisfied. Audit logs record changes to policies, not how they were applied to a sign-in.","Domain 2: Troubleshooting with sign-in logs",["Audit logs record changes to policies, not how they were evaluated for a specific sign-in.","Provisioning logs track account creation in apps, not Conditional Access results.",null,"Secure Score lists posture improvements, not the outcome of one user's sign-in."],2],["x25",0,2,"An analyst verifies that a risky user's account really was compromised. Which ID Protection action records this and raises the user's risk to high?",["Dismiss user risk","Reset the password","Block the user from signing in","Confirm user compromised"],3,"Confirm user compromised sets user risk to high and gives feedback to ID Protection's detections. Dismiss user risk does the opposite, clearing the risk as a false positive.","Domain 2: ID Protection",["Dismissing risk clears it as a false positive, the opposite of confirming compromise.","A password reset remediates risk but does not record that the account was compromised.","Blocking sign-in stops access but gives no feedback to ID Protection and does not set risk to high.",null],2],["x26",0,2,"An admin investigates a high-risk user and finds the detections were caused by legitimate business travel. The user should no longer be blocked by the risk policy. What should the admin do?",["Confirm user compromised","Add the destination to blocked named locations","Dismiss user risk","Delete the user's sign-in log entries"],2,"Dismissing user risk clears the risk state when detections are false positives, so risk-based policies stop blocking the user. Confirming compromise would raise the risk and keep the user blocked.","Domain 2: ID Protection",["Confirming compromise raises the risk to high and keeps the user blocked.","Blocking the destination would stop future business travel and does not clear the current risk.",null,"Sign-in logs cannot be deleted by admins, and deleting them would not change the user's risk state."],2],["x27",0,2,"To publish internal file shares and apps through Microsoft Entra Private Access, what must be installed on a server in the private network?",["A pass-through authentication agent","A private network connector","A Connect Health agent","A Password Protection proxy"],1,"Private Access uses private network connectors in the private network to reach internal resources over outbound connections. A PTA agent validates passwords and has nothing to do with tunneling app traffic.","Domain 2: Global Secure Access",["PTA agents validate passwords against AD and do not carry application traffic.",null,"Connect Health agents monitor sync and AD FS, not tunnel traffic to private apps.","The Password Protection proxy forwards banned password policies to DC agents, not app traffic."],1],["x28",0,2,"Users should be blocked from gambling websites through Microsoft's security service edge, with policies linked to user groups through Conditional Access. What should be used?",["Web content filtering in Entra Internet Access","Quick Access in Microsoft Entra Private Access","A named location listing the gambling site IPs","Cloud discovery reports in Defender for Cloud Apps"],0,"Microsoft Entra Internet Access provides web content filtering by category, applied through security profiles linked to Conditional Access. Private Access Quick Access is for reaching private internal resources, not filtering internet sites.","Domain 2: Global Secure Access",[null,"Quick Access publishes private internal resources; it does not filter internet sites.","Gambling sites use many changing IPs, and named locations describe where users sign in from, not destinations.","Cloud discovery reports on app usage from logs; it does not block websites by category."],2],["x29",0,2,"Access to Microsoft 365 should be allowed only when traffic comes through the company's Global Secure Access tunnel, so stolen tokens cannot be replayed from other networks. What should the Conditional Access policy use?",["A compliant network location condition","A named location with users' home IP addresses","The require approved client app grant control","Continuous access evaluation turned off"],0,"The compliant network check treats traffic arriving through the tenant's Global Secure Access as a location that Conditional Access can require. Home IP named locations are impossible to maintain and do not prove traffic came through the tunnel.","Domain 2: Global Secure Access",[null,"Home IP addresses are impossible to maintain and do not prove traffic came through the tunnel.","Approved client app checks the app used, not whether traffic came through Global Secure Access.","Turning off CAE removes near-real-time enforcement and weakens protection against token replay."],3],["x30",0,2,"Smart card certificate-based authentication is enabled. Sign-ins with those certificates should satisfy Conditional Access policies that require MFA. What should be configured?",["Temporary Access Pass for smart card users","An authentication binding rule set to multifactor","The issuing CA added as a trusted named location","SMS sign-in as a second factor for card users"],1,"CBA authentication binding rules set whether a certificate counts as single-factor or multifactor, so a multifactor binding satisfies MFA requirements. A trusted named location affects location conditions, not the strength of the authentication.","Domain 2: Certificate-based authentication",["A Temporary Access Pass is for onboarding or recovery, not for making certificates count as MFA.",null,"Named locations relate to network location, not the strength of certificate authentication.","Adding SMS introduces a weaker second step instead of letting the smart card itself satisfy MFA."],3],["x31",0,2,"Which of these authentication methods is considered phishing-resistant?",["SMS one-time passcode","Voice call","Windows Hello for Business","Authenticator push notification"],2,"Windows Hello for Business uses keys bound to the device and the site, so it cannot be relayed through a phishing proxy. Push notifications can still be approved by a user tricked by a fake sign-in page.","Domain 2: Authentication methods",["SMS codes can be intercepted or typed into a phishing site, so they are not phishing-resistant.","Voice call codes can be relayed by attackers just like SMS codes.",null,"Push notifications can be approved by a user tricked by a fake sign-in page, so they are not phishing-resistant."],1],["x32",0,3,"A VM's system-assigned managed identity must read blobs in one container and nothing else. What should be assigned?",["Storage Account Contributor at the subscription","The Microsoft Graph permission Files.Read.All","The Directory Readers role in Microsoft Entra ID","Storage Blob Data Reader scoped to that container"],3,"Storage Blob Data Reader is a data-plane role, and scoping it to one container gives least privilege. Storage Account Contributor is a management-plane role at far too broad a scope.","Domain 3: Managed identities",["Storage Account Contributor is a management-plane role at far too broad a scope for reading blobs.","Files.Read.All is a Graph permission for Microsoft 365 files, not Azure Storage blobs.","Directory Readers reads Entra directory objects and grants no storage data access.",null],2],["x33",0,3,"A daemon running outside Azure authenticates with a client secret. It cannot use federated credentials. Which credential is recommended instead for production?",["A longer client secret with the maximum expiry","A shared user account password","A certificate credential","An API key stored in the app manifest"],2,"Certificates are recommended over client secrets for confidential clients because the private key proves possession and is harder to leak casually. A longer-lived secret increases the exposure window rather than reducing risk.","Domain 3: App credentials",["A longer-lived secret increases the exposure window instead of reducing risk.","A shared user account adds interactive credentials to a daemon and cannot be governed properly.",null,"App registrations do not use API keys in the manifest as an authentication credential."],2],["x34",0,3,"An administrator selects 'Grant admin consent' for delegated permissions on an app registration. What is the effect?",["Consent is granted for all users in the tenant","Consent applies only to the admin who clicked it","Application permissions are also granted automatically","Assignment required is switched on for the app"],0,"Admin consent grants the delegated permissions on behalf of all users, so they are not prompted. It covers only the permissions listed, and it does not change the Assignment required setting.","Domain 3: Admin consent",[null,"Admin consent is tenant-wide; it is not limited to the admin who granted it.","Only the permissions listed are consented; application permissions are not added automatically.","Assignment required is a separate enterprise app setting unaffected by consent."],2],["x35",0,3,"An on-premises app published through application proxy must require Microsoft Entra sign-in and Conditional Access before any traffic reaches the internal server. Which setting is required?",["Passthrough pre-authentication","Header-based SSO only","A public DNS record pointing at the server","Microsoft Entra ID pre-authentication"],3,"Microsoft Entra ID pre-authentication makes users sign in and satisfy Conditional Access before the connector forwards traffic. Passthrough sends unauthenticated requests straight to the back-end app.","Domain 3: Application proxy",["Passthrough sends unauthenticated requests straight to the back-end app, bypassing Entra sign-in.","Header-based SSO is a way to pass identity to the app, and still needs Entra pre-authentication to enforce sign-in.","A public DNS record to the server would expose it directly, bypassing application proxy.",null],2],["x36",0,3,"An on-premises app published through application proxy uses Integrated Windows Authentication. Users should not be prompted again after signing in to Microsoft Entra ID. What should be configured?",["SAML-based SSO with a new signing certificate","Integrated Windows Authentication SSO using KCD","Password-based SSO stored in the My Apps portal","Linked SSO pointing to the internal URL"],1,"Application proxy uses Kerberos constrained delegation from the connector to get Kerberos tickets for IWA apps on the user's behalf. SAML SSO would require the app to understand SAML, which an IWA app does not.","Domain 3: Application proxy SSO",["An IWA app does not understand SAML, so SAML SSO cannot sign users in to it.",null,"Password-based SSO replays stored passwords and does not work with Kerberos-based IWA apps.","Linked SSO only links to the app and leaves users facing the app's own Windows authentication prompt."],3],["x37",0,3,"Apps hosted in two separate datacenters are published through application proxy. Each app should be served by connectors located near it. What should be used?",["A separate tenant for each datacenter","Connector groups assigned to each app","One app registration per connector","Staged rollout for each datacenter"],1,"Connector groups let you assign specific connectors to specific apps, such as those in the same datacenter. Separate tenants would split identities and are unnecessary for routing.","Domain 3: Application proxy connectors",["Separate tenants split identities and are unnecessary just to choose which connectors serve an app.",null,"App registrations do not control which connectors carry traffic for an app.","Staged rollout is for moving from federation to cloud authentication, not connector placement."],2],["x38",0,3,"Security wants alerts when an OAuth app granted high-privilege Microsoft Graph permissions suddenly accesses unusual amounts of mail data. Which feature fits?",["Cloud discovery","Microsoft Entra Connect Health","Access reviews of enterprise apps","App governance in Defender for Cloud Apps"],3,"App governance monitors OAuth app permissions and data access behavior and alerts on anomalies. Cloud discovery analyzes network logs for shadow IT, not API activity of consented apps.","Domain 3: Defender for Cloud Apps",["Cloud discovery analyzes network logs for shadow IT, not API activity of consented OAuth apps.","Connect Health monitors sync and AD FS, not OAuth app data access.","Access reviews re-certify who has access periodically but do not detect unusual data access.",null],2],["x39",0,3,"A SaaS app is assigned to a large group, but only users whose department is Sales should be provisioned to it. What should be configured?",["A scoping filter in the provisioning settings","An app role called Sales in the manifest","A Conditional Access policy for the app","Hiding the app from My Apps for others"],0,"Scoping filters limit provisioning to users whose attributes match the rules. A Conditional Access policy controls sign-in but does not affect which accounts are created in the app.","Domain 3: Automatic user provisioning",[null,"App roles are claims for authorization inside the app and do not decide who gets provisioned.","Conditional Access controls sign-in, not which accounts are created in the app.","Hiding the app only removes the tile; assigned users are still provisioned."],2],["x40",0,3,"An enterprise app's provisioning job shows a quarantine status after repeated failures because the app rejects the admin credentials. What should be done?",["Renew the app's SAML signing certificate","Turn off Assignment required for the app","Fix the admin credentials, then restart provisioning","Delete the app and add it again from the gallery"],2,"Quarantine follows persistent failures such as invalid credentials; fixing the secret token or credentials and restarting the job clears it. The SAML certificate is used for sign-in, not the provisioning connection.","Domain 3: Automatic user provisioning",["The SAML certificate is used for sign-in, not the provisioning connection that is failing.","Assignment required affects who can sign in and who is in scope, not credential failures.",null,"Recreating the app loses configuration and would fail again with the same bad credentials."],2],["x41",0,3,"A SaaS app identifies users by email address, but Microsoft Entra ID sends the UPN, which differs from the email for some users. What should be changed in the SAML configuration?",["The Reply URL","The token signing certificate","The Identifier (Entity ID) and the Sign on URL in Basic SAML Configuration","The Unique User Identifier claim, sourced from user.mail"],3,"The Unique User Identifier (NameID) claim determines which attribute the app receives to identify the user, so changing its source to user.mail fixes the mismatch. The Reply URL only controls where the SAML response is sent.","Domain 3: SAML SSO",["The Reply URL controls where the SAML response is sent, not which user attribute it carries.","The signing certificate secures the assertion but does not choose the user identifier attribute.","The Entity ID and Sign on URL identify the app and start sign-in; they do not change the user identifier sent.",null],3],["x42",0,3,"Security wants to stop developers from adding new client secrets to any app registration in the tenant, allowing only certificates or federated credentials. What should be configured?",["Conditional Access for workload identities","'Users can register applications' set to No","A tenant-wide application management policy","The admin consent workflow with reviewers"],2,"Application management policies can restrict credential types, such as blocking new password credentials, across the tenant. Setting 'Users can register applications' to No stops new registrations but not secrets on existing apps.","Domain 3: Securing workload identities",["Conditional Access controls when workload identities can get tokens, not which credential types they can add.","Blocking new registrations does not stop secrets being added to apps that already exist.",null,"The admin consent workflow handles permission requests, not app credential types."],3],["x43",0,3,"A support engineer needs to manage all enterprise apps and app registrations, including application proxy settings. Which built-in role is least privileged for this?",["Cloud Application Administrator","Application Administrator","Application Developer","Global Reader"],1,"Application Administrator manages all aspects of apps including application proxy. Cloud Application Administrator is the same except it cannot manage application proxy, so it falls short here.","Domain 3: Application roles",["Cloud Application Administrator has the same rights except it cannot manage application proxy.",null,"Application Developer can only create registrations and manage apps they own.","Global Reader can view settings but cannot change anything."],3],["x44",0,3,"The team wants ID Protection risk detections for service principals and Conditional Access policies for workload identities. Which license is required?",["Workload Identities Premium","Microsoft Entra ID P1","Microsoft Entra ID Governance","Microsoft 365 E3"],0,"Risky workload identity detections and Conditional Access for workload identities require Workload Identities Premium. Microsoft Entra ID P1 covers Conditional Access for users, not these workload identity features.","Domain 3: Workload identity security",[null,"P1 covers Conditional Access for users, not risk detections or policies for workload identities.","The Governance license covers lifecycle workflows and advanced reviews, not workload identity protection.","Microsoft 365 E3 includes Entra ID P1, which lacks workload identity protection features."],1],["x45",0,3,"An enterprise app must be stopped immediately from letting anyone sign in, but its configuration must be kept for later. What should be done?",["Set 'Enabled for users to sign-in?' to No","Hide the app from users in My Apps","Remove its delegated API permissions","Delete the app registration"],0,"Disabling sign-in for the enterprise app blocks all users from getting tokens while keeping its configuration. Hiding it from My Apps only removes the tile; users can still sign in directly.","Domain 3: Enterprise application management",[null,"Hiding the app removes the My Apps tile, but users can still sign in directly.","Removing permissions affects API access but can still allow users to sign in.","Deleting the app registration destroys the configuration the team wants to keep."],2],["x46",0,3,"After cloud discovery, the team marks an app as unsanctioned and wants managed Windows endpoints to block access to it automatically. What should be set up?",["A Conditional Access policy that blocks that cloud app for all users on all devices","Integration of Defender for Cloud Apps with Defender for Endpoint","A named location containing the app's domain","An access review for users of the app"],1,"With Defender for Endpoint integration, apps marked unsanctioned in Defender for Cloud Apps are blocked on onboarded devices. A Conditional Access policy only applies to apps that sign in through Microsoft Entra ID, which most shadow IT does not.","Domain 3: Defender for Cloud Apps",["Most shadow IT apps do not sign in through Entra ID, so Conditional Access cannot block them.",null,"Named locations describe where users sign in from, not destination apps to block.","An access review re-certifies access periodically and does not block anything on endpoints."],3],["x47",0,4,"The Marketing lead should create and manage access packages for Marketing resources only, without holding a tenant-wide governance role. What should they be given?",["Global Administrator with PIM activation","Ownership of every Marketing group","The catalog owner role on a Marketing catalog","The User Administrator role"],2,"A catalog owner can manage the catalog's resources and access packages without a directory-wide role. Group ownership lets them manage groups, but not create access packages in entitlement management.","Domain 4: Entitlement management",["Global Administrator is far broader than needed, even with PIM activation.","Group owners manage group membership but cannot create access packages in entitlement management.",null,"User Administrator manages users and groups tenant-wide, not access packages in a scoped catalog."],2],["x48",0,4,"When users request an access package, they must enter their project code and a business reason that approvers can see. What should be configured?",["A terms of use grant in Conditional Access","A custom security attribute on each user","A task in a joiner lifecycle workflow that emails the new hire's manager for the details","Requestor questions in the assignment policy"],3,"Access package policies can ask requestors questions, and the answers are shown to approvers. Terms of use require acceptance of a document; they do not collect answers for approvers.","Domain 4: Access packages",["Terms of use require acceptance of a document and do not collect answers for approvers.","Custom security attributes describe users and are set by admins, not entered per request.","A lifecycle workflow runs on HR events, not when users request an access package.",null],2],["x49",0,4,"An access review should go first to each user's manager, then send only the approved users to the app owner for a final decision. What should be used?",["Two separate one-time reviews","A self-review with justification","A multi-stage access review","A PIM approval workflow"],2,"Multi-stage access reviews chain reviewers so later stages can see earlier decisions or review only approved users. Two separate reviews do not pass results from one to the other.","Domain 4: Access reviews",["Two separate reviews do not pass results from the first to the second automatically.","A self-review lets users attest to their own access, with no manager or owner decision.",null,"PIM approval workflows approve role activations, not periodic reviews of app access."],2],["x50",0,4,"Every six months, holders of privileged Microsoft Entra roles must be re-certified by reviewers, and roles removed if not approved. What should be created?",["An access review of Microsoft Entra roles","A PIM alert rule for stale roles","An entitlement management catalog","A scheduled export of the audit logs"],0,"Access reviews can target Microsoft Entra roles through PIM, with recurrence and auto-apply to remove unapproved assignments. PIM alerts flag risky configurations but do not re-certify users.","Domain 4: Access reviews",[null,"PIM alerts flag risky configurations but do not have people re-certify each role holder.","A catalog groups resources for access packages; it does not review directory role assignments.","Exporting audit logs records history but does not remove unapproved role assignments."],2],["x51",0,4,"On an employee's last day, their account should be disabled and removed from groups and licenses automatically, based on the end date HR provides. What should be used?",["A dynamic group with an accountEnabled rule","A recurring access review of all users, with auto-apply set to remove access for anyone not approved","An expiry on PIM eligible assignments","A leaver lifecycle workflow using employeeLeaveDateTime"],3,"Leaver lifecycle workflows trigger on employeeLeaveDateTime and run tasks such as disabling the account and removing groups and licenses. An access review depends on reviewers and does not run on the HR end date.","Domain 4: Lifecycle workflows",["A dynamic group reacts to attributes but cannot disable accounts or remove licenses itself.","An access review depends on reviewers and a schedule, not on the HR end date.","PIM expiry applies only to privileged role assignments, not accounts, groups or licenses.",null],2],["x52",0,4,"A joiner lifecycle workflow must also open a ticket in the IT service desk system for each new hire. How can the workflow do this?",["Send a PIM activation notification","Add a custom task extension that calls a Logic App","Add an approval step in an access package","Use a dynamic group membership rule"],1,"Custom task extensions let lifecycle workflows call Azure Logic Apps to integrate with external systems. An access package approval is a manual approval process, not an integration with a ticketing system.","Domain 4: Lifecycle workflows",["PIM notifications concern role activations and cannot create tickets in an external system.",null,"An access package approval is a manual approval, not an integration with a ticketing system.","Dynamic group rules add members based on attributes and cannot call external systems."],2],["x53",0,4,"The Owner role on a production subscription should be eligible and activated just in time with approval. Which feature manages this?",["PIM for Microsoft Entra roles","PIM for Azure resources","Entitlement management","Conditional Access"],1,"PIM for Azure resources manages Azure RBAC roles such as Owner on subscriptions. PIM for Microsoft Entra roles covers directory roles like Global Administrator, not Azure RBAC.","Domain 4: PIM for Azure resources",["PIM for Microsoft Entra roles covers directory roles, not Azure RBAC roles like Owner.",null,"Entitlement management bundles resources for requests but is not the PIM just-in-time role activation.","Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions, not just-in-time role eligibility."],1],["x54",0,4,"PIM eligible assignments should not last forever; they must end after a set period unless renewed, and users should be able to request an extension. What should be configured?",["An expiration setting in each access package's assignment policy, with extension requests allowed","A sign-in frequency session control","A mover lifecycle workflow","PIM role settings that disallow permanent eligible assignments"],3,"PIM role settings can disallow permanent eligible assignments and set a maximum duration, and users can request extensions before expiry. Access package expiration applies to entitlement management assignments, not PIM role eligibility.","Domain 4: Privileged Identity Management",["Access package expiration applies to entitlement management assignments, not PIM role eligibility.","Sign-in frequency affects how often users re-authenticate, not how long roles are assigned.","Mover workflows react to job changes, not to a fixed eligibility duration.",null],3],["x55",0,4,"Auditors ask who activated Global Administrator last week and what justification they gave. Where is this recorded?",["The PIM audit history for Microsoft Entra roles","The provisioning logs","Identity Secure Score history","Access package assignment and request reports"],0,"PIM's audit history records activations, requests and the justifications entered. Provisioning logs track account provisioning to apps, not role activations.","Domain 4: Privileged Identity Management",[null,"Provisioning logs track account creation in apps, not privileged role activations.","Secure Score history shows posture trends, not who activated a role or why.","Access package reports cover entitlement management, not PIM role activations."],2],["x56",0,4,"A tenant with Microsoft Entra ID P2 relies only on built-in log retention. For how long are sign-in logs kept?",["7 days","90 days","30 days","2 years"],2,"With Microsoft Entra ID P1 or P2, sign-in and audit logs are retained for 30 days. Seven days is the retention for tenants on Microsoft Entra ID Free.","Domain 4: Log retention",["Seven days is the retention for tenants on Microsoft Entra ID Free, not P2.","Ninety days is not the built-in sign-in log retention for P1 or P2 tenants.",null,"Built-in retention is much shorter; longer retention needs export to Log Analytics or storage."],1],["x57",0,4,"An investigator cannot find a daemon app's token requests in the interactive sign-in logs. The app authenticates with its own client credentials. Where should they look?",["Audit logs","Provisioning logs","Non-interactive user sign-ins","Service principal sign-ins"],3,"Apps authenticating as themselves with client credentials appear in service principal sign-in logs. Non-interactive user sign-ins are made by clients on behalf of a user, such as token refreshes, not app-only sign-ins.","Domain 4: Sign-in logs",["Audit logs record changes to directory objects, not app token requests.","Provisioning logs record account provisioning to apps, not sign-ins.","Non-interactive user sign-ins are made on behalf of a user, not by an app with its own credentials.",null],3],["x58",0,4,"Before blocking legacy authentication, the team wants to see which users and apps still use it, from logs sent to Log Analytics. What should they open?",["Identity Secure Score","The PIM alerts page","The legacy authentication sign-ins workbook","The access reviews overview"],2,"The Microsoft Entra workbook for sign-ins using legacy authentication shows which users and apps still use those protocols. Identity Secure Score recommends blocking legacy auth but does not list who uses it.","Domain 4: Workbooks",["Secure Score recommends blocking legacy authentication but does not list who uses it.","PIM alerts are about privileged roles, not legacy protocol usage.",null,"Access reviews re-certify access and give no data on protocols used to sign in."],2],["x59",0,4,"A terms of use document must be accepted separately on every device a user signs in from. Which terms of use setting does this?",["Expire consents on a yearly schedule","Require users to consent on every device","Require users to expand the terms of use","Set a duration before re-acceptance is required"],1,"The 'Require users to consent on every device' setting makes users accept the terms on each device they use. Expiring consents forces re-acceptance over time, not per device.","Domain 4: Terms of use",["Expiring consents forces re-acceptance over time, not on each device.",null,"Expanding the terms makes users open the document before accepting, not accept per device.","A re-acceptance duration sets how often users must accept again, not per device."],2],["x60",0,4,"Sign-in and audit logs are sent to Log Analytics. Which KQL query finds Conditional Access policy changes in the last seven days?",["AuditLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d) | where OperationName has \"conditional access\"","SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d) | where ConditionalAccessStatus == \"failure\"","AuditLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d) | where OperationName has \"Add member to role\"","AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d) | summarize count() by AppId"],0,"Conditional Access policy changes are audit events with operation names such as 'Update conditional access policy', found in AuditLogs. SigninLogs shows how policies were applied to sign-ins, not who changed them.","Domain 4: KQL queries",[null,"SigninLogs shows how policies were applied to sign-ins, not who changed a policy.","That operation name matches role membership changes, not Conditional Access policy updates.","Service principal sign-in logs record app sign-ins and contain no policy change events."],2]]);
